===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The state of a society being without [[authorities]] or an [[authoritative]] [[govern]]ing body.
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[anarchism|Anarchism]]; the political [[theory]] that a [[community]] is best [[organize|organized]] by the [[voluntary]] [[cooperation]] of [[individual]]s, rather than by a [[government]], which is regarded as being [[coercive]] by nature.
# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[chaotic]] and confusing [[absence]] of any form of [[political]] [[authority]] or [[government]].
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1853
|author=w:Leon Faucher
|chapter=IV.
|translator=w:Thomson Hankey
|title=Remarks on the Production of the Precious Metals, and on the Demonetization of Gold in Several Countries in Europe
|url=https://archive.org/details/remarksonproduct00faucrich/
|edition=2nd revised
|location=London
|publisher=w:Smith, Elder, & Co.
|oclc=1235541016
|page=50
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/remarksonproduct00faucrich/page/50/
|text='''Anarchy''' still reigns in this new country;—not only have the miners to defend their persons and their acquisitions against the incursions from Indian tribes; not only are crimes and offences common (lynch law maintaining a permitted existence instead of laws and police); but every one appears to hold his property by right of first comer: a miner choses the spot he likes best; a strong arm and a carbine, with a steady eye, are his title deeds. To seize upon a rich "placer" from a miner too weak to resist, is called in the slang of the district, to "jump a claim." The President of the United States himself, stated in his last message, that "The mineral lands should remain free to every citizen;" and the Secretary of State has added, "that the right of occupancy should be submitted only to such laws as the miners themselves thought fit to make."}}
# [[confusion|Confusion]] in general; [[disorder]].
#: {{ux|en|It was total '''anarchy''' in the clothes shop on Black Friday as soon as they opened the doors.}}

